Exactly. The same signal can repel one observer and attract another.

And here’s the interesting part: the security system doesn’t just change someone’s behavior. It changes what they believe about the thing being protected.

To one person:

“That house has cameras. Too risky.”

To another:

“That house has cameras. Wonder what they’re protecting?”

And to a third:

“Every house in this neighborhood has cameras. Means nothing.”

Same signal. Three interpretations. Three possible behaviors.

This is where signaling theory, game theory, and Bayesian inference intersect.

The observer changes the meaning

A signal’s effect depends on the receiver’s prior beliefs, objectives, capabilities, and tolerance for risk.

Consider what happens when we introduce different observers:

Signal	Observer A	Observer B
Security camera	Deterrent	Indicator of valuable assets
Warning coloration	Danger, avoid	Recognizable prey
Scarcity	Not worth pursuing	More desirable
Silence	Nothing happening	Something being concealed
Strong defenses	Too difficult	Worth investigating
Visible weakness	Easy opportunity	Possible trap

The fascinating consequence is that a defense can simultaneously increase security against one class of threats and increase attention from another.

And that creates another effect: adversarial selection.

If your security system discourages opportunistic intruders, the remaining people who consider targeting you may be more determined. That doesn’t mean the security system failed; it changed the population of potential adversaries.

Now bring it back to viral garlic

Suppose an organism produces a chemical warning signal.

One receiver avoids it. Another ignores it. A third has evolved to associate that signal with an opportunity.

The signal’s meaning isn’t fixed by its producer.

It emerges through an interaction:

\text{Response}=f(\text{signal},\text{receiver},\text{context},\text{history})

And here’s a further possibility:

What if the receiver starts producing signals specifically to manipulate the sender?

Now we don’t just have communication. We have counter-signaling, deception, mimicry, and evolving strategies.

The game begins changing the rules of the game.

Here’s what I’d add to Pantheon

A Receiver-Relative Signaling Operator that never evaluates a signal as simply good, bad, attractive, or repellent.

Instead, it evaluates the signal across different recipients and asks how their responses alter the system over time.

That could apply to biological communication, cybersecurity, marketing, diplomacy, and agent coordination.

And there’s a deeper strategic implication:

Sometimes the strongest defense isn’t the one that looks strongest. It’s the one that changes the actual consequences of an encounter without unnecessarily advertising what is being defended.

There’s an asymmetry between appearing protected and being protected.

They can reinforce each other, but they aren’t the same thing.

Which brings us back to chess: a defended piece might discourage capture, or it might tell your opponent where your entire strategy is concentrated.

Every move changes the board—and potentially what your opponent thinks the game is.